<ChapterLayout
  title="4.3 — Formation and Evolution of Planets · AST 100"
  description="How a spinning disk of gas and dust around the newborn Sun — zoned by temperature, split by snow lines — assembled eight planets in 150 million years: giants first beyond the ice, rocky worlds later, water delivered last."
  activeId="4.3" chapter={4} current="/chapter/4/4.3">
  <Hero
    tease="A star is born inside a spinning disk of gas and dust — and its planets are the leftovers. Temperature wrote the recipe: rock condensed near the fire, ices beyond the snow line, and where ice was plentiful, giant cores grew fast enough to capture gas. Then a hundred million years of collisions — one of which made our Moon — settled eight worlds into the orbits they still keep."
    client:load />

  <article class="topic-page">
    <div class="prose-cosmic lesson-prose">
      <h2 id="disk">A Disk with a Thermostat</h2>
      <p>
        Why are the rocky planets close to the Sun and the giants far away? Why is Earth wet, Mercury bone-dry, and the
        Kuiper belt frozen? Remarkably, one idea answers all of it. In <a href={subPath("3.3")}>§3.3</a> we watched a
        cold cloud of gas collapse under gravity to ignite a star. But a collapsing cloud always spins a little, and as
        it shrinks the spin speeds up — so not everything can fall into the star. The leftover matter flattens into a
        rotating <strong>disk</strong> of gas and dust around the newborn sun, spread across the very plane in which,
        as <a href={subPath("4.1")}>§4.1</a> showed, all the planets still orbit today. The disk <em>is</em> the reason
        the solar system is flat. Astronomers call it the <strong>solar nebula</strong>, and telescopes like ALMA now
        photograph such disks around young stars throughout the galaxy.
      </p>
      <p>
        The disk had a built-in thermostat: blazing near the protosun, frigid at its rim. And temperature decided what
        the local building material was. Closest in, hotter than about 1,400 kelvin, even rock existed only as vapour
        — nothing could grow there at all. A little farther, grains of <strong>rock and metal</strong> could condense,
        but carbon-rich compounds were still burned away inside the so-called <strong>soot line</strong>, leaving the
        inner disk's building blocks dry and carbon-poor. The decisive boundary lay near <strong>2.7 au</strong>: the
        <strong>water snow line</strong>, beyond which water froze onto every grain of dust. Ice roughly doubled the
        amount of solid material there — and farther out still, past about 10 and 27 au, even carbon dioxide and
        carbon monoxide froze. Drag the probe through the young nebula in the figure below and watch the available
        ingredients change with distance; that gradient is the master key to everything that follows.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="The young solar nebula, zone by zone">
        <DiskThermostatPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        The disk's chemistry left fingerprints we can still read. Meteorites — surviving fragments of the first
        solids — come in two chemically distinct families: dry, carbon-poor rock from the inner disk, and carbon- and
        water-rich material from beyond the snow line (the <strong>non-carbonaceous</strong> and
        <strong>carbonaceous</strong> types, NC and CC). Their isotopes show the two reservoirs formed within the
        first million years and then <em>stayed separate</em> for millions more — most plausibly because the growing
        core of Jupiter opened a gap in the disk that acted as a fence. The oldest of these solids, tiny
        calcium-aluminium flecks found inside meteorites, carry radioactive clocks that all read the same age:
        <strong>4,567 million years</strong>. That is the birth date of the solar system, and the "t = 0" of the
        story below.
      </p>

      <h2 id="assembly">One Hundred and Fifty Million Years</h2>
      <p>
        From those first condensed grains to eight finished planets took only about 150 million years — nearly
        nothing against the 4,600 million years since. Growth worked from the bottom up, just as it did for galaxies
        in <a href={subPath("2.3")}>§2.3</a>: microscopic grains stuck together into pebbles, pebbles into
        kilometre-scale <strong>planetesimals</strong>, planetesimals into Moon-to-Mars-sized <strong>embryos</strong>,
        embryos into planets. But the two sides of the snow line ran the race at very different speeds. Beyond it,
        ice-fattened cores grew huge within a couple of million years — quickly enough to gravitationally capture the
        disk's hydrogen and helium <em>before</em> the young Sun's radiation swept the gas away, which it did within
        about 3 to 10 million years. That deadline is why <strong>the giants formed first</strong>, and why Uranus and
        Neptune, growing slower and farther out, missed most of the gas and stayed "ice giants."
      </p>
      <p>
        The inner disk, poor in solids, assembled its worlds the slow, violent way — tens of millions of years of
        collisions between embryos. One of the last of these giant impacts, around 4.5 billion years ago, struck the
        young Earth hard enough to splash a ring of molten debris into orbit: that debris cooled into
        <strong>the Moon</strong>. The same era of upheaval, with the giant planets shifting and scattering small
        bodies across the system, flung water-rich and carbon-rich material from beyond the snow line into the dry
        inner zone — delivering to a parched young Earth the water and organic molecules that would one day fill its
        oceans (the story chapter 5 takes up). By about 150 million years in, the gas was gone, the great collisions
        had tapered off, and the architecture of <a href={subPath("4.1")}>§4.1</a> — rock inside, giants outside,
        rubble and ice in the gaps — was set. Step through the whole construction in the figure below.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Assembling the solar system">
        <AssemblyPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        One caution before we leave home: the tidy picture above is calibrated by a single example. The hot Jupiters of
        <a href={subPath("4.2")}>§4.2</a> proved that planets can <strong>migrate</strong> — a giant born cold beyond
        its snow line can spiral inward to roast beside its star. Our own giants almost certainly wandered too before
        settling, and those wanderings may be why the asteroid belt is so sparse and Mars so small. Every planetary
        system runs the same physics through different dice rolls — which is why the 6,300 systems found around other
        stars, and the methods that found them (<a href={subPath("4.4")}>§4.4</a>), matter so much: they are the
        experiment repeated.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <p><em>A spinning disk, zoned by temperature; ice beyond 2.7 au; giants first, rocky worlds later; water
        delivered by the leftovers. In 150 million years the blueprint was done — and it has held for 4.4 billion
        more. Next: how we catch other stars running the same experiment.</em></p>
    </div>
  </article>

